// Repo-local markdownlint config. run-standards.sh prefers this over the
// canonical fleet config when it is present, so anything set here applies to
// CI as well as local runs.
//
// IMPORTANT: a repo-local config REPLACES the canonical one rather than
// extending it, so the `config` block below must mirror
// github-workflows/standards/markdownlint.json. Without it this repo would
// silently pick up rules the fleet has turned off (MD013 line-length being the
// noisy one) and diverge from the standard in ways unrelated to the
// grandfathering this file exists for. If the canonical config changes, this
// block needs the same change.
//
// Why these 64 files are ignored:
//
// This repo is a detached fork of obra/superpowers. GitHub no longer records a
// fork parent, but the history does: of the 64 Markdown files listed below,
// every one was written upstream -- 415 commits by Jesse Vincent, 42 by Drew
// Ritter, and a long tail of other contributors. Not one was authored by the
// owner of this fork. The only Markdown file here that is his own work is
// CLAUDE.md, and it already passes, so it is deliberately absent from this
// list and stays linted.
//
// Fixing the 775 findings by hand would mean reformatting several dozen files
// of someone else's prose -- other people's skill docs, design specs, and
// release notes -- to satisfy a linter this fork adopted after the fact. That
// is a rewrite of upstream writing, not a cleanup, and it would make every
// future merge from upstream conflict. So the pre-existing debt is
// grandfathered and the standard is enforced on everything else.
//
// Why an explicit file list rather than a directory glob:
//
// The debt is diffuse, so no glob is narrower than this list. Ignoring
// `skills/**` and `docs/**` -- the two obvious candidates -- covers only 54 of
// the 64 dirty files, still leaves README.md, RELEASE-NOTES.md,
// CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, GEMINI.md, tests/ and .github/ failing, and swallows 23
// files that are already clean. That would leave markdownlint scanning 2 files
// out of 89, which is switching the linter off, not grandfathering. Listing
// the files individually keeps the other 25 clean files under the full fleet
// ruleset, and any new Markdown file is linted from the day it is added.
//
// Why not disable the offending rules instead:
//
// The debt is not uniform enough for a rule-level cut. MD032 accounts for 439
// findings across 52 files and MD031 another 202 across 33, but the tail is
// spread thin -- MD024, MD025, MD026, MD038 and MD056 appear in a single file
// each. Reaching a clean run by disabling rules would take all 14, globally,
// including in files that do not need it. A path-scoped ignore keeps every
// rule live everywhere else.
//
// Maintenance: this list only ever shrinks. If you substantively edit one of
// these files, delete its entry and fix its findings in the same change.
// Note that markdownlint-cli2 does not warn when an ignore pattern matches
// nothing, so an entry for a file that was renamed or deleted upstream will
// sit here silently -- prune it when you notice it.
{
  "ignores": [
    ".github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md",
    ".github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/feature_request.md",
    ".github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/platform_support.md",
    ".github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md",
    ".opencode/INSTALL.md",
    "CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md",
    "docs/plans/2025-11-22-opencode-support-design.md",
    "docs/plans/2025-11-22-opencode-support-implementation.md",
    "docs/plans/2025-11-28-skills-improvements-from-user-feedback.md",
    "docs/plans/2026-01-17-visual-brainstorming.md",
    "docs/README.opencode.md",
    "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-01-22-document-review-system.md",
    "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-02-19-visual-brainstorming-refactor.md",
    "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-03-11-zero-dep-brainstorm-server.md",
    "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-03-23-codex-app-compatibility.md",
    "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-04-06-worktree-rototill.md",
